    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "abXMH",
      "x": 6097.485354840993,
      "y": -143.84896888594847,
      "width": 740,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "Polity Democracy Civic Engagement\n\n**Polity** refers to a system of government or a political order, encompassing the institutions, structures, and processes through which power is exercised and decisions are made. It is a broad term that can describe any form of governance, including democracies, autocracies, or monarchies.\n\n**Democracy** is a specific type of polity characterized by rule by the people, either directly or through elected representatives. Core elements include free and fair elections, protection of human rights, the rule of law, separation of powers, and political pluralism. In a democracy, citizens have the right and opportunity to participate in shaping public policy and holding leaders accountable.\n\n**Civic engagement** is the participation of individuals and groups in activities that address public concerns and contribute to the well-being of their communities. It includes both political actions—such as voting, protesting, or running for office—and non-political actions like volunteering, community organizing, or participating in local forums. While **democracy depends on civic engagement** to function effectively, civic engagement is broader in scope and includes all forms of community involvement that promote collective well-being and social responsibility. As noted by scholars, **democratic engagement is a key component of civic engagement**, focusing specifically on participation in democratic processes."
    }
  ]
}